=== input 0's tests run once, with a summary
let twice(n: Int): Int { n * 2 }; test("doubles") { assert(twice(2) == 4) }; test("zero") { assert(twice(0) == 0) }
--- transcript
Tests passed: 2 of 2
interactive#0: void

=== a failing test says why, and the declarations still work
let twice(n: Int): Int { n * 2 }; test("ok") { assert(twice(1) == 2) }; test("off") { assert(twice(2) == 5) }; test("msg") { assert(false) { "says why" } }
twice(21)
--- transcript
Test failed: off - expected twice(2) == (5) not (4)
Test failed: msg - says why
Tests passed: 1 of 3
interactive#0: void
interactive#1: 42

=== input 0's tests and declarations, used afterwards; later tests refuse
class Point(public x: Int, public y: Int) {}; let origin = new Point(0, 0); test("origin") { console.log("in a test"); assert(origin.x == 0) }
new Point(origin.x + 1, 2)
test("t") { assert(true) }
origin.y
--- transcript
in a test
Tests passed: 1 of 1
